00:09:26,730 --> 00:09:27,890
meaning the instruction set,

277
00:09:27,890 --> 00:09:29,490
what exactly does it define?

278
00:09:29,490 --> 00:09:31,550
Let's just list it briefly here.

279
00:09:31,550 --> 00:09:34,430
Later on, when we get to the details,

280
00:09:34,430 --> 00:09:35,370
we'll go through them one by one.

281
00:09:35,370 --> 00:09:36,890
Let me briefly explain here.

282
00:09:36,890 --> 00:09:39,130
For example, it defines some basic data types.

283
00:09:39,130 --> 00:09:41,790
For instance, this byte.

284
00:09:41,790 --> 00:09:43,230
Or what a word is.

285
00:09:43,230 --> 00:09:45,470
These names actually come from the microarchitecture,

286
00:09:45,470 --> 00:09:48,110
and they're defined for you from the instruction set perspective.

287
00:09:48,110 --> 00:09:50,270
For example, one byte is 8 bits,

288
00:09:50,270 --> 00:09:50,490
right?

289
00:09:50,490 --> 00:09:51,890
How much is one word?

290
00:09:51,890 --> 00:09:55,070
It's 32 bits,

291
00:09:55,070 --> 00:09:55,490
correct?

292
00:09:55,490 --> 00:09:56,390
A halfword.

293
00:09:56,390 --> 00:09:58,210
We'll run into this concept a lot later on.

294
00:09:58,210 --> 00:09:59,730
A halfword is 16 bits.

295
00:09:59,730 --> 00:10:00,810
They're all multiples of 8.

296
00:10:00,810 --> 00:10:03,430
What else is there?

297
00:10:03,430 --> 00:10:05,050
What else does the ISA define?

298
00:10:05,050 --> 00:10:05,870
Registers.

299
00:10:05,870 --> 00:10:06,910
Because as we said,

300
00:10:06,910 --> 00:10:09,350
when we interact with the hardware to operate it,

301
00:10:09,350 --> 00:10:10,650
When it comes to the CPU,

302
00:10:10,650 --> 00:10:12,570
it is mainly about operating its registers.

303
00:10:12,570 --> 00:10:13,690
For example, this

304
00:10:13,690 --> 00:10:15,170
given architecture,

305
00:10:15,170 --> 00:10:16,470
that is, on this CPU,

306
00:10:16,470 --> 00:10:18,010
how many kinds of registers it implements,

307
00:10:18,010 --> 00:10:19,490
those have also been standardized.

308
00:10:19,490 --> 00:10:21,630
Then what each register is for,

309
00:10:21,630 --> 00:10:21,890
and so on.

310
00:10:21,890 --> 00:10:22,970
Then there is

311
00:10:22,970 --> 00:10:24,010
a third thing,

312
00:10:24,010 --> 00:10:25,410
which is what everyone cares about most,

313
00:10:25,410 --> 00:10:25,850
and that is instructions.

314
00:10:25,850 --> 00:10:27,450
Because when we write assembly,

315
00:10:27,450 --> 00:10:29,650
it is mainly programming,

316
00:10:29,650 --> 00:10:31,230
and how each of its

317
00:10:31,230 --> 00:10:32,690
statements is written out.

318
00:10:32,690 --> 00:10:33,950
That is really the concept of instructions.

319
00:10:33,950 --> 00:10:35,810
Then there are addressing modes,

320
00:10:35,810 --> 00:10:37,470
and how exceptions and interrupts are handled.

321
00:10:37,470 --> 00:10:37,930
and so on and so forth

322
00:10:37,930 --> 00:10:39,170
Let me just list a few things here.

323
00:10:39,170 --> 00:10:40,130
So the main thing we want to...

324
00:10:40,130 --> 00:10:41,770
This is mainly to get a better feel for...

325
00:10:41,770 --> 00:10:42,110
That is, what is...

326
00:10:42,110 --> 00:10:43,450
What I mean is, an instruction set architecture...

327
00:10:43,450 --> 00:10:44,310
is a standard.

328
00:10:44,310 --> 00:10:45,230
It defines a lot of things...

329
00:10:45,230 --> 00:10:46,210
rather than just being simply...

330
00:10:46,210 --> 00:10:47,750
an assembly language.

331
00:10:47,750 --> 00:10:48,450
So everyone...

332
00:10:48,450 --> 00:10:51,550
This slide is mainly to emphasize this point to you.

333
00:10:51,550 --> 00:10:54,890
Now, as we said...

334
00:10:54,890 --> 00:10:58,230
why do we design this instruction set architecture?

335
00:10:58,230 --> 00:10:59,310
Actually, we mentioned it just now.

336
00:10:59,310 --> 00:11:00,250
It's the concept of layering.

337
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:00,850
Think about it, isn't that right?

338
00:11:00,850 --> 00:11:02,390
Once we have this thing...

339
00:11:02,390 --> 00:11:05,230
we can say that for the upper-layer software...

340
00:11:05,230 --> 00:11:06,150
so this upper-layer software...

341
00:11:06,150 --> 00:11:08,210
It actually refers to writing an operating system,

342
00:11:08,210 --> 00:11:09,890
this kind of low-level stuff like a bootloader,

343
00:11:09,890 --> 00:11:12,190
the developers involved in this kind of work.

344
00:11:12,190 --> 00:11:13,750
They also do software,

345
00:11:13,750 --> 00:11:16,010
so they don't need to care about

346
00:11:16,010 --> 00:11:17,570
the underlying circuit structure.

347
00:11:17,570 --> 00:11:18,430
And that circuit structure

348
00:11:18,430 --> 00:11:19,590
is what we just talked about,

349
00:11:19,590 --> 00:11:20,670
the concept of microarchitecture.

350
00:11:20,670 --> 00:11:24,450
We actually just interact with the underlying layer through a standard set of interfaces,

351
00:11:24,450 --> 00:11:27,110
and that's all we need to do.

352
00:11:27,110 --> 00:11:29,610
Now, on this diagram,

353
00:11:29,610 --> 00:11:30,350
let me point something out to you,

354
00:11:30,350 --> 00:11:32,130
which is that we should also understand

355
00:11:32,130 --> 00:11:34,970
the development history of this RSA instruction set architecture.

356
00:11:34,970 --> 00:11:36,970
That is, the first to propose

357
00:11:36,970 --> 00:11:39,050
this instruction set architecture

358
00:11:39,050 --> 00:11:40,150
was who?

359
00:11:40,150 --> 00:11:41,790
It was the company IBM.

360
00:11:41,790 --> 00:11:44,310
Do you all know the full name of IBM?

361
00:11:44,310 --> 00:11:49,560
that is, International Business Machines

362
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:52,100
it was actually originally a hardware company

363
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:52,720
it made machines

364
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:53,780
you can tell just from the name

365
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:55,540
it made computers

366
00:11:55,540 --> 00:11:58,540
so back when it was doing this

367
00:11:58,540 --> 00:12:01,860
probably in the last century

368
00:12:01,860 --> 00:12:03,240
which decade was that again

369
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:03,920
I don't know, the 60s

370
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:05,180
or maybe the 70s

371
00:12:05,180 --> 00:12:07,200
so when it was developing

372
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:09,100
this kind of computer

373
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:11,240
being a pioneer, it was like this

374
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:12,680
before there were instruction set architectures

375
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:15,240
as everyone knows, for every model of computer

376
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:16,660
its processor

377
00:12:16,660 --> 00:12:19,080
its operating interface

378
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:20,140
was different

379
00:12:20,140 --> 00:12:21,000
say this computer

380
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,460
there were, for example, 10 computers

381
00:12:22,460 --> 00:12:24,180
That company had 20 computers

382
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:25,580
and each computer served a different purpose.

383
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:27,300
So things were pretty chaotic.

384
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:28,480
Now, for IBM,

385
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:30,660
by that time they had already grown to the point

386
00:12:30,660 --> 00:12:32,000
where their company's products

387
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:33,580
had reached three or four models,

388
00:12:33,580 --> 00:12:36,280
and they found this problem was getting more and more serious.

389
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:36,740
Think about it,

390
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:38,140
because of this—

391
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:41,160
since the underlying interfaces were different,

392
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:42,480
they had to have four teams,

393
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:43,500
at least four teams.

394
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:45,660
The software teams had to develop different

395
00:12:45,660 --> 00:12:46,780
compilers,

396
00:12:46,780 --> 00:12:49,960
operating systems, and so on.

397
00:12:49,960 --> 00:12:52,800
So the cost was just too high for them.

398
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:56,440
So starting with the IBM 360 machine—

399
00:12:56,440 --> 00:12:58,980
this machine, which is this one in this picture—

400
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:01,440
let's take a look at what early computers looked like.

401
00:13:01,440 --> 00:13:03,900
This is the machine

402
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:09,360
and it really doesn't look much like our computers today.

403
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:10,100
Do you all see that?

404
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:12,540
There are so many buttons on it.

405
00:13:12,540 --> 00:13:14,320
You can think of these buttons as,

406
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:16,500
how should I put it,

407
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:18,420
this thing is actually

408
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:19,880
the early concept of the console.

409
00:13:19,880 --> 00:13:22,260
You know, our computers today have what's called a

410
00:13:22,260 --> 00:13:22,820
Console,

411
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:23,960
that console,

412
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:26,340
the one where you're always typing away at the keyboard,

413
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:28,140
that input interface.

414
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:29,200
Don't we call that the console?

415
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:30,480
Well, the early console

416
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:31,740
was really just this thing.

417
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:33,620
It had a lot of buttons on it,

418
00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:34,720
for controlling the computer,

419
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:35,740
for making it do things,

420
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:37,300
switches and so on.

421
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:40,120
So this machine is called the 360.

422
00:13:40,120 --> 00:13:42,340
So IBM, starting from this machine,

423
00:13:42,340 --> 00:13:44,680
defined the first instruction set architecture.

424
00:13:44,680 --> 00:13:45,800
And what it defined,

425
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:47,840
is basically what we just talked about:

426
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:49,620
what registers there are,

427
00:13:49,620 --> 00:13:50,700
these instructions,

428
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:53,820
and also the concept of a byte.

429
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:55,920
So in all the computing we've done so far,

430
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:56,720
this concept of a byte

431
00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:58,800
actually started with this computer.

432
00:13:58,800 --> 00:13:59,420
Before that,

433
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:01,140
everyone's standards were kind of all over the place.

434
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:03,600
So starting from this computer,

435
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:04,740
we defined it:

436
00:14:04,740 --> 00:14:05,500
one byte

437
00:14:05,500 --> 00:14:06,700
is eight bits.

438
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:08,600
This standard was set right there,

439
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:09,600
and we've been using it ever since.

440
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:10,980
That's how it is.

441
00:14:10,980 --> 00:14:12,320
That's how history played out.

442
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:17,300
So it created a kind of abstraction between hardware and software.

443
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:23,720
So after RISC came along,

444
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:26,180
we're still in that situation today.

445
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:30,800
Alright, so now that we understand what an instruction set architecture is,

446
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:33,000
and why instruction set architectures were invented in the first place,

447
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,740
let's look at some concepts related to instruction set architectures.

448
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:39,040
Because they'll come up from time to time later on,

449
00:14:39,040 --> 00:14:40,320
so we should get familiar with them.

450
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:43,120
So mainly what I want to talk about here

451
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:44,220
is the classification

452
00:14:44,220 --> 00:14:45,840
of instruction set architectures.

453
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:47,460
Typically,

454
00:14:47,460 --> 00:14:49,840
this is actually covered in many textbooks

455
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:51,100
as well,

456
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:52,720
the typical split is into two types:

457
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:53,820
one is called CISC,

458
00:14:53,820 --> 00:14:54,820
and the other is called RISC.

459
00:14:54,820 --> 00:14:56,060
So CISC,

460
00:14:56,060 --> 00:14:57,740
its full name is

461
00:14:57,740 --> 00:15:00,720
Complex Instruction Set Computing.

462
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:02,320
In other words, the complex instruction set.

463
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:03,760
Now, RISC

464
00:15:03,760 --> 00:15:07,200
stands for Reduced Instruction Set Computing,

465
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:08,320
which is the reduced instruction set.

466
00:15:08,320 --> 00:15:09,800
Now, the one we'll talk about later,

467
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:10,600
that RISC-5,

468
00:15:10,600 --> 00:15:12,100
you can tell just from the name

469
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:12,880
which category our RISC-5

470
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:13,760
belongs to.

471
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:16,140
It falls under this classic instruction set category.

472
00:15:16,140 --> 00:15:18,300
Now, the main

473
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:22,260
difference between the two, where does it lie?

474
00:15:22,260 --> 00:15:23,260
It's written up here too.

475
00:15:23,260 --> 00:15:25,220
With the complex instruction set,

476
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:26,040
you can think of it this way:

477
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:29,900
its instructions,

478
00:15:29,900 --> 00:15:31,620
its...

479
00:15:31,620 --> 00:15:34,220
you can understand it like this:

480
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:35,500
in order to implement,

481
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:36,300
Take a computer, for example,

482
00:15:36,300 --> 00:15:37,380
it has many functions, right?

483
00:15:37,380 --> 00:15:39,320
And for each of these functions,

484
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:41,540
you design a dedicated instruction

485
00:15:41,540 --> 00:15:43,580
to go with it.

486
00:15:43,760 --> 00:15:46,700
Now, as you can imagine, as computers develop,

487
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:47,940
and their functions become more and more complex,

488
00:15:47,940 --> 00:15:50,320
a certain trend is bound to emerge.

489
00:15:50,320 --> 00:15:53,960
That is, when the CPU is implemented based on a complex instruction set,

490
00:15:53,960 --> 00:15:56,340
built around this concept,

491
00:15:56,340 --> 00:15:58,380
the number of instructions it supports grows,

492
00:15:58,380 --> 00:15:59,620
and keeps getting larger and larger,

493
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:00,780
more and more bloated, right?

494
00:16:00,780 --> 00:16:03,680
Now, a classic instruction set

495
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:05,660
takes a different approach.

496
00:16:05,660 --> 00:16:07,420
The idea is to use a few—

497
00:16:07,420 --> 00:16:09,740
to define a set of common, basic instructions,

498
00:16:09,740 --> 00:16:11,260
and then for more complex functions,

499
00:16:11,260 --> 00:16:12,420
you use those basic instructions—

500
00:16:12,420 --> 00:16:14,480
combining them through addition, subtraction, multiplication, division

501
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:15,580
Like these basic instructions,

502
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:16,600
you put them together.

503
00:16:16,600 --> 00:16:18,000
So this way,

504
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,500
it keeps the instructions... that is, the number of instructions,

505
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:22,180
the number of them,

506
00:16:22,180 --> 00:16:23,220
the variety,

507
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:24,400
from growing too fast.

508
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:27,700
Now, some of you might ask,

509
00:16:27,700 --> 00:16:30,180
why does this happen?

510
00:16:30,180 --> 00:16:31,560
This is also for historical reasons,

511
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:33,780
because back in the early days,

512
00:16:33,780 --> 00:16:35,160
actually the first ones to come out were

513
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:36,760
the complex instruction set computers.

514
00:16:36,760 --> 00:16:37,980
They came out early on.

515
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:39,240
So why is that?

516
00:16:39,240 --> 00:16:40,060
It's mainly because,

517
00:16:40,060 --> 00:16:41,740
as you know, in that era,

518
00:16:41,740 --> 00:16:43,080
inside a computer,

519
00:16:43,080 --> 00:16:45,140
what was the most precious resource?

520
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:48,500
Actually, even looking at it now,

521
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:50,060
In fact, this phenomenon still exists,

522
00:16:50,060 --> 00:16:54,740
that is, its memory is extremely precious,

523
00:16:54,740 --> 00:16:56,440
because back then, given the manufacturing process

524
00:16:56,440 --> 00:16:57,140
and cost issues,

525
00:16:57,140 --> 00:16:58,880
a computer, like the one we just saw,

526
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:01,220
inside such a complex computer,

527
00:17:01,220 --> 00:17:03,180
its memory could be pitifully small.

528
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:06,160
That was caused by the conditions at the time.

529
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:08,240
So, since memory was scarce,

530
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:08,840
then, as you know,

531
00:17:08,840 --> 00:17:09,960
when writing programs,

532
00:17:09,960 --> 00:17:10,680
what did they have to do?

533
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:12,200
They had to be extremely frugal.

534
00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:12,720
Right?

535
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:14,560
If I want to implement a very complex function,

536
00:17:14,560 --> 00:17:17,100
the program I write to generate this,

537
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:18,120
it has to be as short as possible.

538
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:21,880
Because the storage,

539
00:17:21,880 --> 00:17:25,200
probably, even on a mainframe like this,

540
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:27,840
it was probably only a few tens of...

541
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:29,420
I'm not sure if it ever reached the megabyte level

542
00:17:29,420 --> 00:17:30,840
it was pitifully small

543
00:17:30,840 --> 00:17:33,020
We might not really feel this nowadays,

544
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:34,600
but back in the day, even a single byte

545
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:36,500
had to be saved.

546
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:38,340
So think about it — given that starting point,

547
00:17:38,340 --> 00:17:40,680
they wanted the instructions

548
00:17:40,680 --> 00:17:41,740
that is, the code you write,

549
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:43,500
the machine instructions generated from it,

550
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:45,320
to be as short as possible,

551
00:17:45,320 --> 00:17:46,660
so every single instruction

552
00:17:46,660 --> 00:17:47,560
had to be fully utilized

553
00:17:47,560 --> 00:17:48,900
to accomplish a specific function.

554
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:49,480
Think about it — isn't that the case?

555
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:52,000
So naturally, this phenomenon emerged.

556
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:53,600
And later on,

557
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:55,860
as our technology advanced,

558
00:17:55,860 --> 00:17:56,320
as we know,

559
00:17:56,320 --> 00:17:57,820
memory

560
00:17:57,820 --> 00:18:00,020
and everything became cheap.

561
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:02,140
Now, as for the size of this program,

562
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:03,820
we actually don't care that much anymore.

563
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:05,320
What we care about more is what?

564
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:06,620
What we care about more is

565
00:18:06,620 --> 00:18:08,140
its,

566
00:18:08,140 --> 00:18:12,620
for us programmers, a kind of

567
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:13,760
comprehensibility,

568
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:14,640
readability,

569
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:15,700
including that,

570
00:18:15,700 --> 00:18:16,960
this compiler,

571
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:17,720
its,

572
00:18:17,720 --> 00:18:19,280
since the compiler is also getting more and more complex,

573
00:18:19,280 --> 00:18:21,040
then we can actually design this instruction

574
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:21,860
to be very simple,

575
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:23,080
and let the compiler help us

576
00:18:23,080 --> 00:18:24,920
assemble these complex functions.

577
00:18:24,920 --> 00:18:25,620
Don't you think so?

578
00:18:25,620 --> 00:18:26,940
This,

579
00:18:26,940 --> 00:18:28,560
or let's say,

580
00:18:28,560 --> 00:18:29,860
let me give you an example from our daily life.

581
00:18:29,860 --> 00:18:31,500
Actually, this might make it easier for everyone to understand.

582
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:32,180
You all know,

583
00:18:32,180 --> 00:18:33,940
for example, take Chinese characters.

584
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:36,480
You've all studied Chinese in school, right?

585
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:38,020
In the early days of Chinese writing,

586
00:18:38,020 --> 00:18:39,760
classical Chinese versus modern vernacular Chinese,

587
00:18:39,760 --> 00:18:40,580
what's the relationship there?

588
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:41,980
Well, it's actually kind of similar to,

589
00:18:41,980 --> 00:18:43,100
the idea of complex instruction sets versus,

590
00:18:43,100 --> 00:18:44,700
simple instruction sets.

591
00:18:44,700 --> 00:18:46,400
With the classical Chinese of the past,

592
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:46,780
as you know,

593
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:48,020
a single Chinese character had to convey,

594
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:48,880
a really complex meaning.

595
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:49,800
Think about it, isn't that right?

596
00:18:49,800 --> 00:18:50,440
Why?

597
00:18:50,440 --> 00:18:51,800
Why did our Chinese,

598
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:52,580
our ancestors,

599
00:18:52,580 --> 00:18:53,460
design our classical writing,

600
00:18:53,460 --> 00:18:55,020
to be like that?

601
00:18:55,020 --> 00:18:57,020
Because as everyone knows,

602
00:18:57,020 --> 00:18:58,980
in the early days, paper was too expensive.

603
00:18:58,980 --> 00:19:00,720
And it wasn't even paper — what was it?

604
00:19:00,720 --> 00:19:02,580
It was those bamboo strips,

605
00:19:02,580 --> 00:19:03,700
that kind of thing, right?

606
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:05,120
In the West, they didn't even have that.

607
00:19:05,120 --> 00:19:06,360
They wrote on parchment and so on.

608
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:08,180
Only highly educated people could write.

609
00:19:08,180 --> 00:19:10,260
Common people simply had no chance to write at all.

610
00:19:10,260 --> 00:19:11,980
So since these bamboo strips

611
00:19:11,980 --> 00:19:13,940
were so complicated to produce,

612
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:16,700
every single character had to fully express a meaning.

613
00:19:16,700 --> 00:19:17,400
That is to say, each character

614
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:19,140
packed in a particularly rich set of meanings.

615
00:19:19,140 --> 00:19:21,600
We used to talk about that saying,

616
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:23,480
the one about 'sweating oxen crossing the ford' —

617
00:19:23,480 --> 00:19:24,740
or 'learning rich enough to fill five carts'.

618
00:19:24,740 --> 00:19:26,720
Do you know how many books five carts could hold?

619
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:28,140
Actually, those books didn't have that many characters.

620
00:19:28,140 --> 00:19:28,820
Do you all know that?

621
00:19:28,820 --> 00:19:29,900
In the past, educated people

622
00:19:29,900 --> 00:19:31,920
probably didn't read all that many books in their whole lives.

623
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:34,300
But because every character carries so many meanings,

624
00:19:34,300 --> 00:19:36,020
the amount of information conveyed was actually quite large.

625
00:19:36,020 --> 00:19:37,240
Then later on,

626
00:19:37,240 --> 00:19:38,980
with vernacular writing and cheaper paper,

627
00:19:38,980 --> 00:19:41,520
the cost of learning kept dropping,

628
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:43,160
and that's how vernacular writing came about.

629
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:44,280
Why use vernacular writing?

630
00:19:44,280 --> 00:19:45,600
Simply because it's easier to understand.

631
00:19:45,600 --> 00:19:46,600
Think about it, isn't that right?

632
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:47,540
So this classic instruction set,

633
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:50,020
the trend now is to develop toward the classic instruction set.

634
00:19:50,020 --> 00:19:52,300
And there's a similar reason behind that, too.

635
00:19:52,300 --> 00:19:53,560
Of course, the two of them

636
00:19:53,560 --> 00:19:54,520
are also converging with each other.

637
00:19:54,520 --> 00:19:55,240
So this one,

638
00:19:55,240 --> 00:19:57,380
if we look at it from a historical perspective,

639
00:19:57,380 --> 00:19:58,460
there is this classification,

640
00:19:58,460 --> 00:20:00,340
and that is the complex instruction set.

641
00:20:00,340 --> 00:20:01,920
the concept of a classic instruction set

642
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:04,900
Now, the RISC-V we'll be learning later on,

643
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:05,540
should be a

644
00:20:05,540 --> 00:20:07,480
it's also a standard classic instruction set.

645
00:20:07,480 --> 00:20:10,020
As for complex instruction sets,

646
00:20:10,020 --> 00:20:11,200
early on, for example, Intel's

647
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:12,540
processor,

648
00:20:12,540 --> 00:20:14,700
it was, from the very beginning, a complex instruction set.

649
00:20:14,700 --> 00:20:15,320
And now,

650
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:18,860
it's also slowly shifting towards a classic instruction set.

651
00:20:18,860 --> 00:20:22,510
So that's what we just talked about—

652
00:20:22,510 --> 00:20:23,950
the classification of instruction sets.

653
00:20:23,950 --> 00:20:25,210
Now the next one,

654
00:20:25,210 --> 00:20:27,010
is about this instruction set's

655
00:20:27,010 --> 00:20:28,230
concept of width.

656
00:20:28,230 --> 00:20:31,610
Because when we buy computers these days,

657
00:20:31,610 --> 00:20:33,750
we actually often care about this too,

658
00:20:33,750 --> 00:20:34,910
like, say, this notion of 32-bit

659
00:20:34,910 --> 00:20:36,090
and 64-bit.

660
00:20:36,090 --> 00:20:38,830
Well, this actually has to do with the instruction set.

661
00:20:38,830 --> 00:20:40,930
And later, when we write code,

662
00:20:40,930 --> 00:20:42,750
we'll keep bringing up this concept too.

663
00:20:42,750 --> 00:20:45,850
So now we need to be clear on,

664
00:20:45,850 --> 00:20:48,450
what exactly does this width refer to?

665
00:20:48,450 --> 00:20:51,630
When we say this width, what does it actually mean?

666
00:20:51,630 --> 00:20:53,210
It actually refers to the CPU—

667
00:20:53,210 --> 00:20:57,070
the general-purpose registers in the processor,

668
00:20:57,070 --> 00:20:58,070
their width.

669
00:20:58,070 --> 00:20:59,710
We know that a register,

670
00:20:59,710 --> 00:21:01,530
you can think of it as basically a storage unit,

671
00:21:01,530 --> 00:21:03,470
individual storage units,

672
00:21:03,470 --> 00:21:05,570
storage units inside the CPU.

673
00:21:05,570 --> 00:21:07,410
This concept—our concept of registers—

674
00:21:07,410 --> 00:21:08,690
in the diagram from last class,

675
00:21:08,690 --> 00:21:09,570
go back and take another look at it.

676
00:21:09,570 --> 00:21:10,510
Where does it sit?

677
00:21:10,510 --> 00:21:11,870
What role does it play inside?

678
00:21:11,870 --> 00:21:13,330
Still remember?

679
00:21:13,330 --> 00:21:14,790
Think back to what we went through last class,

680
00:21:14,790 --> 00:21:16,010
the one I demonstrated to you.

681
00:21:16,010 --> 00:21:17,290
the execution process of a program

682
00:21:17,290 --> 00:21:18,950
what role does that register play in it

683
00:21:18,950 --> 00:21:20,250
because as we said,

684
00:21:20,250 --> 00:21:22,630
when the CPU is actually processing data,

685
00:21:22,630 --> 00:21:24,210
it can't just directly take that

686
00:21:24,210 --> 00:21:25,670
data out of memory

687
00:21:25,670 --> 00:21:26,290
and start using it right away.

688
00:21:26,290 --> 00:21:27,850
It first has to take the data in memory

689
00:21:27,850 --> 00:21:30,650
and put it into the registers inside the CPU,

690
00:21:30,650 --> 00:21:31,870
and only then can it work on it

691
00:21:31,870 --> 00:21:32,770
from the registers.

692
00:21:32,770 --> 00:21:33,290
Right?

693
00:21:33,290 --> 00:21:35,330
So registers are extremely important.

694
00:21:35,330 --> 00:21:37,310
Now, the size of this register

695
00:21:37,310 --> 00:21:38,270
determines the

696
00:21:38,270 --> 00:21:39,590
so-called

697
00:21:39,590 --> 00:21:41,070
width of the ISA instruction set,

698
00:21:41,070 --> 00:21:42,570
and it actually also determines

699
00:21:42,570 --> 00:21:44,190
the processing power of this CPU.

700
00:21:44,190 --> 00:21:45,730
Now, a typical

701
00:21:45,730 --> 00:21:46,930
Now, as for the size of this register,

702
00:21:46,930 --> 00:21:47,730
we've listed it here:

703
00:21:47,730 --> 00:21:49,910
there are 8-bit, 16-bit, 32-bit, and 64-bit.

704
00:21:49,910 --> 00:21:50,890
What that actually refers to is,

705
00:21:50,890 --> 00:21:52,250
a 'bit' is just one binary digit.

706
00:21:52,250 --> 00:21:53,170
One binary digit,

707
00:21:53,170 --> 00:21:54,190
one bit,

708
00:21:54,190 --> 00:21:55,890
which is a 0 or a 1 — one bit.

709
00:21:55,890 --> 00:21:57,950
Now over here,

710
00:21:57,950 --> 00:21:58,670
I've just laid out a simple list,

711
00:21:58,670 --> 00:21:59,490
meaning that one 8-bit value...

712
00:21:59,490 --> 00:22:01,290
and you'll often need to have this

713
00:22:01,290 --> 00:22:01,970
in your head,

714
00:22:01,970 --> 00:22:03,190
you need to be familiar with this kind of calculation,

715
00:22:03,190 --> 00:22:05,410
because in low-level development,

716
00:22:05,410 --> 00:22:06,610
you're very often dealing with individual bits,

717
00:22:06,610 --> 00:22:07,910
calculating bit by bit,

718
00:22:07,910 --> 00:22:08,470
and encoding things.

719
00:22:08,470 --> 00:22:10,450
So these numbers,

720
00:22:10,450 --> 00:22:12,130
you should all get quite familiar with them going forward.

721
00:22:12,130 --> 00:22:13,550
256, 6536

722
00:22:13,550 --> 00:22:15,010
But that second number is too long

723
00:22:15,010 --> 00:22:15,810
so we won't memorize it.

724
00:22:15,810 --> 00:22:18,810
But these are all powers of 2.

725
00:22:18,810 --> 00:22:22,170
What do they look like in hexadecimal?

726
00:22:22,170 --> 00:22:23,790
When you see these numbers,

727
00:22:23,790 --> 00:22:25,510
you should develop a kind of professional instinct.

728
00:22:25,510 --> 00:22:27,470
What do I mean by professional instinct?

729
00:22:27,470 --> 00:22:30,490
You'll gradually cultivate this sense over time.

730
00:22:30,490 --> 00:22:31,950
Then low-level development

731
00:22:31,950 --> 00:22:33,530
will probably become easier to get into.

732
00:22:33,530 --> 00:22:36,630
Now, as for 8-bit CPUs,

733
00:22:36,630 --> 00:22:37,850
in the early days they were definitely all...

734
00:22:37,850 --> 00:22:39,470
There were even 4-bit ones,

735
00:22:39,470 --> 00:22:40,670
for example, from Intel,

736
00:22:40,670 --> 00:22:43,510
there was a chip called 4004.

737
00:22:43,510 --> 00:22:45,210
You can tell from the 4 at the beginning that it's 4-bit.

738
00:22:45,210 --> 00:22:46,550
As for the 8-bit ones,

739
00:22:46,550 --> 00:22:48,730
they were actually the first to become more practical.

740
00:22:48,730 --> 00:22:49,690
So we say

741
00:22:49,690 --> 00:22:51,870
The classic 8-bit machine is what

742
00:22:51,870 --> 00:22:52,790
the 8051.

743
00:22:52,790 --> 00:22:54,050
As for the 8051,

744
00:22:54,050 --> 00:22:56,130
I don't know if you guys at school now

745
00:22:56,130 --> 00:22:58,870
still cover this chip in class.

746
00:22:58,870 --> 00:23:00,390
I think it should probably be phased out by now,

747
00:23:00,390 --> 00:23:01,370
because it's just too old.

748
00:23:01,370 --> 00:23:02,390
Really too old.

749
00:23:02,390 --> 00:23:03,450
But back in our day,

750
00:23:03,450 --> 00:23:04,530
when we were learning microcontrollers,

751
00:23:04,530 --> 00:23:05,550
this chip was still around,

752
00:23:05,550 --> 00:23:06,070
and we actually used this one,

753
00:23:06,070 --> 00:23:07,410
because this chip was so famous,

754
00:23:07,410 --> 00:23:08,130
it was used incredibly widely,

755
00:23:08,130 --> 00:23:10,130
both internationally and,

756
00:23:10,130 --> 00:23:11,170
in particular, here in China.

757
00:23:11,170 --> 00:23:12,970
So it's an 8-bit machine,

758
00:23:12,970 --> 00:23:14,230
meaning that inside it,

759
00:23:14,230 --> 00:23:14,990
each register

760
00:23:14,990 --> 00:23:16,210
is only 8 bits wide.

761
00:23:16,210 --> 00:23:18,290
It can only read 8 bits at a time.

762
00:23:18,290 --> 00:23:19,350
That much data into it.

763
00:23:19,350 --> 00:23:20,710
So you can imagine its processing power.

764
00:23:20,710 --> 00:23:23,770
Then later it became 16-bit.

765
00:23:23,770 --> 00:23:24,410
With 16-bit,

766
00:23:24,410 --> 00:23:25,750
the more typical ones

767
00:23:25,750 --> 00:23:27,610
are the 8086,

768
00:23:27,610 --> 00:23:29,310
and also MSP,

769
00:23:29,310 --> 00:23:31,350
which are some microcontrollers from TI.

770
00:23:31,350 --> 00:23:33,610
So for something like the 8086,

771
00:23:33,610 --> 00:23:35,230
what we call x86, x86,

772
00:23:35,230 --> 00:23:37,310
actually started from the 8086 generation.

773
00:23:37,310 --> 00:23:39,830
8086, 80286, 386, 486.

774
00:23:39,830 --> 00:23:40,730
That's where it comes from.

775
00:23:40,730 --> 00:23:42,370
Now, this 8086,

776
00:23:42,370 --> 00:23:43,310
was the earliest one,

777
00:23:43,310 --> 00:23:44,610
and it was actually still 16-bit.

778
00:23:44,610 --> 00:23:45,950
Then later,

779
00:23:45,950 --> 00:23:48,010
moving on to the 286 and 386,

780
00:23:48,010 --> 00:23:50,070
32-bit ones started to appear.

781
00:23:50,070 --> 00:23:51,730
So at that time,

782
00:23:51,730 --> 00:23:54,130
after we entered the 32-bit era,

783
00:23:54,130 --> 00:23:55,590
our computers and the like,

784
00:23:55,590 --> 00:23:57,090
that era started to arrive,

785
00:23:57,090 --> 00:23:58,510
the PC started to come along.

786
00:23:58,510 --> 00:23:59,830
And now,

787
00:23:59,830 --> 00:24:01,890
we can say that the mainstream processors,

788
00:24:01,890 --> 00:24:03,850
have actually all transitioned to 64-bit.

789
00:24:03,850 --> 00:24:04,370
Now, as we know,

790
00:24:04,370 --> 00:24:05,750
when it comes to the kind of

791
00:24:05,750 --> 00:24:07,150
chips we buy these days,

792
00:24:07,150 --> 00:24:08,790
their 64-bit

793
00:24:08,790 --> 00:24:09,470
actually refers to

794
00:24:09,470 --> 00:24:12,270
the width of their general-purpose registers,

795
00:24:12,270 --> 00:24:13,310
which is 64 bits.

796
00:24:13,310 --> 00:24:15,770
The range that a single 64-bit number

797
00:24:15,770 --> 00:24:16,630
can represent,

798
00:24:16,630 --> 00:24:17,910
that is quite astounding.

799
00:24:17,910 --> 00:24:19,770
Right now, the mainstream devices,

800
00:24:19,770 --> 00:24:21,650
including our phones

801
00:24:21,650 --> 00:24:23,510
I won't say anything about PCs

802
00:24:23,510 --> 00:24:24,350
And then there are servers

803
00:24:24,350 --> 00:24:27,150
Those are generally 64-bit

804
00:24:27,150 --> 00:24:28,190
Now some of you might say,

805
00:24:28,190 --> 00:24:31,650
why don't we have 128-bit yet?

806
00:24:31,650 --> 00:24:33,910
Indeed, we don't

807
00:24:33,910 --> 00:24:34,570
Unfortunately

808
00:24:34,570 --> 00:24:39,510
Strictly speaking, there is no 128-bit general-purpose computer,

809
00:24:39,510 --> 00:24:40,570
no 128-bit general-purpose processor

810
00:24:40,570 --> 00:24:42,070
Not yet

811
00:24:42,070 --> 00:24:42,970
The reason is very simple

812
00:24:42,970 --> 00:24:44,710
It's just not practical yet

813
00:24:44,710 --> 00:24:46,110
Because human needs

814
00:24:46,110 --> 00:24:47,490
haven't reached that level

815
00:24:47,490 --> 00:24:49,190
I remember reading an article,

816
00:24:49,190 --> 00:24:49,750
someone said

817
00:24:49,750 --> 00:24:51,230
that even if you take

818
00:24:51,230 --> 00:24:53,130
all the atoms

819
00:24:53,130 --> 00:24:54,150
on the entire Earth

820
00:24:54,150 --> 00:24:54,810
and add them all up,

821
00:24:54,810 --> 00:24:56,610
it's not much bigger than 128 bits

822
00:24:56,610 --> 00:24:59,110
the number of values it can represent isn't that much larger

823
00:24:59,110 --> 00:25:00,730
what this really means is

824
00:25:00,730 --> 00:25:01,770
there's just no real possibility

825
00:25:01,770 --> 00:25:04,390
of doing computations with 128-bit data

826
00:25:04,390 --> 00:25:05,930
but then again, it's hard to say for sure

827
00:25:05,930 --> 00:25:08,690
maybe as technology advances

828
00:25:08,690 --> 00:25:11,450
128-bit will come along at some point too

829
00:25:11,450 --> 00:25:14,910
for now, at least, let's not worry about any of that

830
00:25:14,910 --> 00:25:16,550
later on, when we

831
00:25:16,550 --> 00:25:18,950
when we actually build the operating system,

832
00:25:18,950 --> 00:25:20,550
we'll choose a 32-bit system

833
00:25:20,550 --> 00:25:21,510
64-bit

834
00:25:21,510 --> 00:25:24,750
the reason we choose a 32-bit system

835
00:25:24,750 --> 00:25:26,590
is really just to make the calculations easier for you

836
00:25:26,590 --> 00:25:27,630
when you do those calculations

837
00:25:27,630 --> 00:25:28,210
